> 1) for numerical indicators, the lambda for one the indicators is set to one.

also for categorical indicators

> 2) for categorical indicators ... the threshold for the same indicator is set to zero.

No, not in lavaan or Mplus.  The default is to estimate all thresholds and fix the latent item-response intercept to zero and (marginal or conditional, i.e., total or residual) variance to 1.  You can trade whatever estimates you want, but all identification methods lead to statistically equivalent models.  See Wu & Estabrook (2016) for a lot more detail in the multigroup case.

https://doi.org/10.1007/s11336-016-9506-0

Also my slides for the summary version.  Go to my faculty page (link below) and find the Workshops tab.  The first bullet point has slides that explain a lot of the arbitrary trading we can do to identify these models, and tries to draw parallels to how we identify distributions of common factors.

